<DocsLayout
  title="Tools & policy"
  eyebrow="Capabilities"
  description="The built-in tool surface, tool contributors, and policy modes."
  prev={{ href: "/docs/openrouter/", title: "OpenRouter" }}
  next={{ href: "/docs/local-hooks/", title: "Local hooks" }}
>
  <p>
    Tools are registered through <code>ToolContributor</code> and executed through <code>ToolExecutor</code>. Providers only see <code>ToolSpec</code> schemas; the runtime
    owns policy checks, approval events, execution context, and result routing.
  </p>

  <h2>Built-in coding tools</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>read_file</code> and <code>list_files</code> for scoped filesystem reads. Relative paths resolve from the workspace root; <code>user://</code> maps to <code>$HOME</code> and <code>workspace://</code> maps to the workspace root.</li>
    <li><code>grep</code> and <code>glob</code> for workspace discovery. With unrestricted filesystem access, <code>glob</code> also accepts absolute patterns outside the workspace. Both tools understand <code>user://...</code> and <code>workspace://...</code> paths.</li>
    <li><code>write_file</code>, <code>edit</code>, and <code>multi_edit</code> for edit-oriented models, using the same relative, absolute, home, <code>user://</code>, and <code>workspace://</code> path handling as the read tools.</li>
    <li><code>apply_patch</code> for patch-oriented models, including the Responses freeform custom-tool channel for GPT-5.5.</li>
    <li><code>view_image</code> for validated local or remote-runner image inspection by vision-capable models, including canonical skill and workspace image paths.</li>
    <li><code>echo</code> for deterministic tests and simple harness checks.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Process tools</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>exec_command</code> starts an interactive command session and returns either output or a session id.</li>
    <li><code>write_stdin</code> sends input to an existing command session and polls recent output.</li>
    <li><code>unified_exec</code> exposes the Codex-shaped single-tool session contract over the same persistent process pool.</li>
    <li><code>shell</code> runs a single shell command and returns aggregated output.</li>
  </ul>

  <p>
    Process tools honor the configured command shell and the current turn deadline. In
    eval-profile runs, long process timeouts are capped before the finalization reserve so
    the model can still write a final answer or scoreable output.
  </p>

  <p>
    Runner-bound workspaces use the same timeout semantics. Remote one-shot
    {" "}<code>exec_command</code> calls are normalized to a finite effective deadline before
    the provider request is built, so the outer wait, remote process lease, and returned
    timeout metadata describe the same bound. Shared remote-runner sessions also serialize
    workspace tool operations behind the provider's execution fence, preventing two agents
    from interleaving a multi-step file or shell operation in the same sandbox.
  </p>

  <h2>Tool allowlists</h2>
  <p>
    Canonical path aliases are accepted before tool policy is applied. This lets skills
    hand a model paths such as <code>user://.codex/skills/review/SKILL.md</code> or
    {" "}<code>workspace://.agents/skills/roadmap/SKILL.md</code> without hard-coding the
    current machine's home directory or repo checkout.
  </p>

  <p>
    Distributions can narrow the advertised tool set with <code>[tools].allowlist</code>.
    The runtime filters tool specs before they are sent to the model, which is useful for
    headless task-specific builds that should not expose generic editing or shell tools.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`[tools]
allowlist = ["zerolang_skills_get", "zerolang_check", "zerolang_edit"]`}</code></pre>

  <h2>Local hooks</h2>
  <p>
    Projects can add <a href="/docs/local-hooks/">local hooks</a> in
    <code>.roder/hooks.json</code>, with <code>.codex/hooks.json</code> as a compatibility
    fallback. Hooks can observe session start/end, prompts, permission requests, tool
    calls, compaction, subagents, and stop events. <code>SessionStart</code> setup runs
    once for the opened thread session, whether the session started fresh or resumed
    saved state. <code>PreToolUse</code> hooks can deny a tool call or rewrite its input
    before normal execution continues; hook failures are recorded as diagnostics and
    fail open.
  </p>

  <h2>Browser goals and decisions</h2>
  <p>
    <a href="/docs/browser-decisions/">Jev and OpenAI Decisions</a> run bounded browser
    goals in a thread's Chrome session. Results include semantic DOM structure, roles,
    control states and action evidence. Opt-in DOM profiles can use viewport images
    and completion audits; approvals, origin restrictions and secret handling remain
    active. Add observable success conditions and inspect the final page before treating
    a browser task as complete.
  </p>

  <h2>Extension tools</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>web_search</code> from the web-search router extension.</li>
    <li><code>parallel_search</code> and <code>parallel_extract</code> when Parallel.ai is the selected web-search provider or namespaced web tools are enabled.</li>
    <li><code>task</code> from the subagent extension, with optional per-agent namespaced tools.</li>
    <li><a href="/docs/agent-swarm/"><code>agent_swarm</code></a> for bounded fanout of one prompt template across many items.</li>
    <li><code>knowledge_*</code> tools for markdown-backed project knowledge documents.</li>
    <li><code>media_generate_image</code>, <code>media_attach</code>, and related media artifact tools.</li>
    <li><code>zerolang_*</code> graph-inspection and checked-edit tools from the Zerolang extension.</li>
    <li>MCP tools loaded by <code>roder-ext-mcp</code> from configured streamable HTTP servers.</li>
    <li>Provider or product-specific tools installed by custom extensions.</li>
  </ul>

  <h2>Parallel web access</h2>
  <p>
    With <code>[web_search]</code> set to provider <code>parallel</code>, the canonical
    {" "}<code>web_search</code> tool routes through Parallel.ai and Roder also installs
    {" "}<code>parallel_extract</code>. Search requests send the objective, optional focused
    keyword queries, max result count, domain include/exclude filters, location, and
    mode through Parallel's current V1 <code>advanced_settings</code> request shape.
  </p>

  <pre><code>{`[web_search]
enabled = true
mode = "external"
provider = "parallel"

[web_search.parallel]
enabled = true
api_key = "..."`}</code></pre>

  <p>
    <code>parallel_extract</code> fetches clean markdown from known URLs after search has
    found the best sources. It accepts <code>url</code> or <code>urls</code>, an
    {" "}<code>objective</code> or <code>query</code>, optional search-query focus, and
    bounded content limits. When these tools are present, Roder injects short developer
    guidance telling the model to search first, extract the best sources when snippets are
    not enough, and cite URLs for factual claims.
  </p>

  <h2>Workspace and fork controls</h2>
  <p>
    Clients can branch agent work without teaching models about provider internals.
    <a href="/docs/workspace-forks/">Workspace forks</a> expose thread and workspace fork
    methods, while tool execution still runs through the active policy mode and scoped
    workspace handles.
  </p>

  <h2>Policy modes</h2>
  <ul>
    <li><code>default</code> - writes, process, and network are allowed subject to approval policy.</li>
    <li><code>accept_edits</code> - edit tools are auto-approved while other risky operations still pass through policy.</li>
    <li><code>plan</code> - blocks writes and process execution, requires user approval to exit.</li>
    <li><code>bypass</code> - auto-approves all tools and emits a bypass warning event.</li>
  </ul>

  <pre><code>cargo run -p roder-cli -- --mode plan
cargo run -p roder-cli -- --mode accept-edits
cargo run -p roder-cli -- --yolo</code></pre>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Edit-tool selection.</strong> Roder intentionally exposes either patch-style
    editing or direct edit tools for a model request, not both, to keep model behavior predictable.
    See <a href="/docs/edit-tools/">the edit tools package</a> for the reusable
    JavaScript package boundary.
  </div>

  <div class="callout">
    <strong>Codex-trained tool shapes.</strong> See <a href="/docs/codex-parity-tools/">Codex-parity tools</a> for <code>view_image</code>, <code>unified_exec</code>, freeform <code>apply_patch</code>, and eval-profile persistence knobs.
  </div>
</DocsLayout>
